  "query_preview": "..\nNext 7 days (through Thursday)...\n\nSatellite imagery showed an upper low over the Pacific NW with an\nupper ridge over the central CONUS resulting in a brisk southwest\nmid level flow over our region. Downslope flow is helping to \nboost temps and dry out the atmosphere. Afternoon temps should top \nout from mid 80s west to upper 90s east. Minimum humidity \nreadings will range from upper teens west to near 10 percent over \nour eastern plains. A Red Flag is in place across our east and \nsoutheast sections through through early evening. Folks are urged \nto take care and not cause any sparks! \n\nConditions will remain hot and mostly dry into Saturday but a\nchange to surface winds out of the east should allow minimum\ndaytimes humidities to remain mostly in the 20s and 30s percent. \n\nThe next best chance for precipitation arrives by Sunday evening \nas the upper trough to the west tracks into the inter-mountain \nRockies increasing the southwest flow over our area as energy\nejects around the bottom of the trough across Montana. The upper\ntrough is progged by models to track northeastward across Montana\ninto Monday with associated large scale lift producing showers \nand thunderstorms Sunday night through Monday. For our CWA...the \nbest chance of notable rainfall is across our western zones (think\nYellowstone west and north) with less chance toward the southeast\ncorner of Montana. Some storms during this time may produce heavy\nrain as PWATS will be pushed above one inch over the lower \nelevations. We do not anticipate any widespread severe storms, but\ncannot rule out locally strong winds and perhaps small hail. \n\nTemperatures should gradually trend cooler over the weekend into \nnext week as the upper trough approaches and exits the region. \nLook for highs in the 70s over the west to 80s over the east by \nMonday.\n\nOnce the upper low exits our region, we will see some modest \nridge building by mid week leading to mainly dry conditions and\nseasonable temperatures. BT",
